Hash Tables have a Worst Case of O(n), and a Best Case of O(1)

Collisions within a Hash Table are what lead to a worst case

Hash Tables need a good Hash Function in order to ensure little to no Collisions
of Key values

Hash Tables use Key-Value Pairing
The key will be put through a hash function, in order to produce an index 
where the value will be stored (the more optimized a Hash Function, the less clustering
of indexes, which will decrease the chance of collision)

The Hash Function will always require Modulus by the size of the Hash Table

Collision and The Load Factor are what determine the performance of a Hash Table

Hash Tables have two main functions
put(key, value)
get(value)

Open Addressing (Linear Probe))- Resizing 
If the Load Factor is over 0.75, you need to resize and 
double the capacity to reduce the Load Factor
After resizing, you need to re-hash the Key, and if there is collision, you will take a
colliding hash value and place it in an empty Index of the array
until there's no more collision
Requires you store the Key alongside the Value, since all colliding entries share
the same Hash Index Value


Separate Chaining - Hash Table should never be full
Treats every index in the array as the head of a node. When
a Collision occurs, all of the Hashes are kept in the same Linked List
The key will be used to differentiate the Hashes in this Linked List
This Linked List of a Hash Index Value is called a "Bucket"


Hash Tables do NOT maintain the order of The Array Values when they were added




